  "step_2": "The individual considers the probability of the negative outcome in Lottery B: '0.1 wird nicht eintreten' (0.1% will not occur). This indicates awareness of the low probability of the large loss. The individual then states 'also lieber kein Geld verlieren' (so better not lose any money), which reflects a preference for avoiding any loss, even a small one. The deliberation focuses on the low likelihood of the large loss and the desire to avoid any financial loss. The individual does not mention the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, nor does they reference the relative concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. Instead, the justification centers on avoiding loss and the perceived unlikelihood of the worst outcome.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness—specifically, whether a lottery assigns a greater portion of probability to favorable outcomes. Lottery B is more left-skewed than Lottery A, so the reason would favor B. However, the individual’s reasoning does not reference skewness or the distribution of probability across outcomes. Instead, the individual focuses on the low probability of the large loss and a desire to avoid any loss at all. This suggests the individual is using a risk-aversion or loss-avoidance heuristic, possibly focusing on the certainty of a small loss in A versus the possibility of a large loss in B. The individual does not compare the concentration of probability on favorable outcomes, which is central to the skewness reason. Therefore, the individual’s justification does not align with the skewness-based rule. The information considered (probability of large loss, desire to avoid loss) is not the same as that relevant to the skewness reason. The justification also does not match the rule: the reason would prefer B (more left-skewed), but the individual’s reasoning implies a preference for A (avoiding even a small loss). Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

